---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { link } from "../lib/paths";
import { PROBEHARBOR } from "../lib/site";
import { AFFILIATE_DISCLOSURE } from "../lib/shop";
import { PROJECTS } from "../data/projects";

const tested = PROJECTS.filter((p) => (p.verified ?? []).length > 0).length;
---

<Base
  title="About"
  current="about"
  description="What ESP32 Bench is, how projects are tested, what is stored in your browser, and how buy links work."
>
  <section class="view prose">
    <h1 class="h2">About ESP32 Bench</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      ESP32 Bench is a free workbench for ESP32 boards that runs in your browser. Pick a project, see exactly which pins
      it uses on your board, copy the sketch or flash prebuilt firmware, then watch the board talk on the serial bench.
      When something breaks, the crash translator and the error reference explain what the board is saying.
    </p>

    <h2>How projects are tested</h2>
    <p>
      Each project page says how far it has been checked. {tested} of the {PROJECTS.length} projects {tested === 1 ? "has" : "have"} checks
      done on real hardware, listed with the board and the firmware they were run on. The others say "Not hardware tested":
      their sketches follow the documented Arduino-ESP32 APIs but nobody has watched them run on a bench yet. If you
      build one, the serial output it prints is the best test there is.
    </p>

    <h2>What stays in your browser</h2>
    <p>
      There are no accounts. Visits are counted with Ahrefs Web Analytics and Cloudflare Web Analytics, which record
      page views, referrers and rough location without cookies; they never see what you type or anything from your board. Your bench log, badges,
      saved board passports, theme and serial settings are kept in this browser's local storage and never leave it. Serial data goes from the board to this page and
      nowhere else. Wi-Fi details you type into a probe's settings form are sent to the
      board over USB only.
    </p>

    <h2 id="affiliate">Buy links</h2>
    <p>{AFFILIATE_DISCLOSURE} Prices and stock are whatever the shop says on the day; the parts lists name the part, so any equivalent will do.</p>

    <h2>Browsers</h2>
    <p>
      Reading projects works everywhere. Connecting to a board, flashing and the detective use Web Serial, which Chrome,
      Edge and Opera support on desktop computers. Firefox and Safari do not support it. The
      <a href={link("/start/")}>getting started guide</a> covers drivers and permissions.
    </p>

    <h2>Install it</h2>
    <p>
      In Chrome or Edge, use the Install app button in the header, or the install icon in the address bar. The
      installed app opens in its own window, and pages you have opened work without a connection.
    </p>

    <h2>ProbeHarbor</h2>
    <p>
      The hardware-tested pins, the probe firmware, the ProbeHarbor sketches and several failures in the Hall of
      Flames come from <a href={PROBEHARBOR.url}>ProbeHarbor</a>, {PROBEHARBOR.blurb}. Its code is on
      <a href={PROBEHARBOR.repo}>GitHub</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>License</h2>
    <p>
      The code, including every sketch on the site, is MIT licensed. The written content and data are licensed
      <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>: reuse them with a credit and a link to
      esp32bench.dev.
    </p>

    <h2>Safety</h2>
    <p>
      Pin numbers on this site are GPIO numbers, and boards differ. Check your own board's pinout before powering
      anything, keep mains voltage out of hobby projects unless you know exactly what you are doing, and never feed a
      5 V signal into an ESP32 pin.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact and corrections</h2>
    <p>
      Found a wrong pin, a broken link or a bug? Every page has a "Report a mistake on this page" link at the bottom.
      Built a project? Its page has a link to send a build report; working reports become the hardware checks shown on
      that page. Anything else, use the <a href={link("/report/?kind=message")}>contact form</a>. Everything sent
      through these forms goes by email to the person who runs the site and is never published. The form uses
      Cloudflare Turnstile to keep bots out.
    </p>

    <p class="when">See <a href={link("/changes/")}>what's new</a> for the release history.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
